it over to both of you,

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:54.720
so we can include it
in the liner notes.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:55.260
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:57.593
MYLES BORINS: But someone
wrote all this amazing content

00:08:57.593 --> 00:09:00.030
and donated it to the
website redesign group.

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:04.140
And it was to live on the
/learn page on that new site.

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:07.457
And when we found out that we
were going to get the .dev TLD,

00:09:07.457 --> 00:09:09.790
one of the first things we
needed to figure out was, OK,

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:12.390
well, what content are we
going to even put there?

00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:14.010
And it quickly came off as--

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:16.680
hey, actually, we have
all these wire frames

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:20.730
for the new, redesigned website,
including this one learn page.

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:24.540
And we have 70 to 100
pages of documentation.

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:26.140
Why don't we just do that?

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:28.350
And so we actually just
made was originally

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:31.050
supposed to be a single
route on the new page

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:34.470
into this one
specific landing page.

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:36.840
And it's possible that
we may just slowly

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:39.090
bootstrap more and more
features onto this page.

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:42.240
And it could eventually become
the new Node.js landing page

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:43.050
altogether.

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:45.510
But it may continue to just
be its own separate learning

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:46.350
resource page.

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:48.570
But Jon, to more explicitly
answer your question,

00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:50.258
all of this is very
much up in the air.

00:09:50.258 --> 00:09:52.800
And it's going to be very much
determined by who's showing up

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:55.560
and who's doing the work,
what work actually gets done,

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:57.690
what kind of consensus
we can build around it.

00:09:57.690 --> 00:09:59.400
But the future is open.

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:01.680
It can be whatever the
people who are working on it

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:02.457
make it into.

00:10:02.457 --> 00:10:03.540
JON FOUST: That's awesome.

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:05.190
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, and it
looks like all the content

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:06.330
is all available on GitHub.

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:08.705
So if you want to go and edit
it, you can go play with it

00:10:08.705 --> 00:10:11.340
all in there, if you wanted to
add or edit and do stuff to it.

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:13.170
MYLES BORINS: Yeah, and we
actually just added, today,

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:14.250
at the bottom of
the page-- it's just

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:16.125
an Edit on GitHub button,
which will drop you

00:10:16.125 --> 00:10:17.408
right into the GitHub Editor.

00:10:17.408 --> 00:10:18.700
And so it's all just mark down.

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:20.070
So if you see a
spelling mistake,

00:10:20.070 --> 00:10:21.630
just scroll down to the
bottom of the page--

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:22.050
MARK MANDEL: Fix it.

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:22.530
MYLES BORINS:
--click that button.

00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:23.250
You could send us a PR.

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:24.160
JON FOUST: Awesome.

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:24.990
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:26.370
And I think you
were talking earlier

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:28.470
and you said there was some
Cloud Build integration

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:29.170
or something like that.

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:30.540
You did some integration there.

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:31.510
What's that all about?

00:10:31.510 --> 00:10:33.552
MYLES BORINS: Yeah-- so
for those who don't know,

00:10:33.552 --> 00:10:36.390
Cloud Build is a
product from GCP.

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:39.120
I have been, very
recently-- as of yesterday,

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:40.590
when I had an aha moment--

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:43.530
referring to it
as serverless ops.

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:46.470
But it's a really cool product
where, with a YAML file,

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:49.140
you can describe-- hey,
here's a container--

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:51.150
and then run this command
with the container

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:52.800
and set a series of steps.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:57.150
You can then connect Cloud
Build to a particular repo.

00:10:57.150 --> 00:10:59.910
And then you can trigger
particular YAML files--

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:02.340
like, Describe Builds
or Docker files--

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:04.110
on specific actions.

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:07.170
And those can either be
pushes to specific branches,

00:11:07.170 --> 00:11:10.330
the creation of tags,
or pull requests.

00:11:10.330 --> 00:11:12.000
And you can create
regular expressions

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:13.170
to filter them all, as well.

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:16.110
So you could be like,
hey, if a branch is

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:17.920
pushed that matches this--

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:20.160
so for example, one of
the things that we do

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:23.223
is deploy to Firebase
Hosting on a push to master.

00:11:23.223 --> 00:11:25.140
So it auto-deploys with
continues integration.

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:26.160
MARK MANDEL: Oh, nice.

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:28.327
MYLES BORINS: And then the
other one that we have is

00:11:28.327 --> 00:11:31.230
build and push to staging
on a pull request.

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:34.050
But one of the things that Cloud
Build has that's interesting

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:34.620
is--

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:37.080
we have it set up so that
it doesn't run automatically

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:38.670
when a pull request is opened.

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:41.507
A collaborator actually has
to put in a special comment.

00:11:41.507 --> 00:11:42.840
And that will trigger the build.

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:43.500
MARK MANDEL: Oh, I see.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:45.250
MYLES BORINS: And the
reason for that is--

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:48.450
what gets neat with this is,
internally, in the IAM roles

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:52.380
inside of Google Cloud, you
can set specific IAM roles

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:54.220
to the build agent itself.

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:57.630
So for example, I'm using Cloud
Build to deploy to Firebase.

00:11:57.630 --> 00:12:00.750
And all I had to do
was deploy a new image

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:03.360
that is meant to deploy
with the Firebase API.

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:06.810
And then I gave Cloud Build
itself IAM Admin privileges

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:08.340
over Firebase.

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:10.770
And now, whenever
it runs that script,

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:14.580
all of the credentials that are
necessary are already in-lined.

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:16.830
So I don't need to
inline any credentials.

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:20.610
And I can restrict or
add more privileges,

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:21.900
based on what the need are.

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:23.692
But this is part of
the reason to have it--

00:12:23.692 --> 00:12:25.680
so that, for running
the build, you

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:27.968
need to have a comment
from a collaborator.

00:12:27.968 --> 00:12:29.760
We went back and forth
on this quite a bit.

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:32.010
But it could be, like,
a resource attack.

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:34.200
If you just automatically
kicked it off

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:36.360
and then someone could
just, like, shimmy

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:37.230
in a bitcoin miner--

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:38.650
[DIGITAL CLING]

00:12:38.650 --> 00:12:40.255
--which you don't
want happening.

00:12:40.255 --> 00:12:41.520
MARK MANDEL: Ooh-- so
that's really interesting.

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:43.145
How have you got the
integration set up

00:12:43.145 --> 00:12:46.800
between the GitHub page itself
and writing a comment there

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:48.900
and then, like, firing off
a build on Cloud Build?

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:50.543
Because they're not
hooked up that way.

00:12:50.543 --> 00:12:52.710
MYLES BORINS: So this is
just built into Cloud Build

00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:53.160
actually.

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:53.720
MARK MANDEL: Oh, yeah?

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:55.050
MYLES BORINS: So
with Cloud Build,

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:56.640
there's a new thing
that's rolling out

00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:59.130
publicly soon, where you
essentially click a button

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:00.540
to link the repo.

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:02.610
It links the pull request.

00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:05.460
And using GitHub
event-based API,

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:08.400
it actually triggers an event,
which we handle internally

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:09.450
on every comment.

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:11.340
So it's actually
just a box you tick

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:12.010
when you're setting up the--

00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:12.180
MARK MANDEL: Oh, that's cool.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:14.347
MYLES BORINS: --pull request
trigger that just says,

00:13:14.347 --> 00:13:17.290
do not run this trigger unless
someone explicitly runs it.

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:19.440
So we actually have
two different things

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:20.340
going on right now.

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:23.640
We have one check suite,
where Travis runs.

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:25.650
And Travis will run the build.

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:27.180
Travis will run the test suite.

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:29.370
And it will let us know
if anything's broken.

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:30.860
And then we can
trigger this other

00:13:30.860 --> 00:13:32.390
build through Cloud Build.

00:13:32.390 --> 00:13:34.670
And what it will do is
a compiles the site.

00:13:34.670 --> 00:13:35.390
It builds it.

00:13:35.390 --> 00:13:36.650
We have a single folder.

00:13:36.650 --> 00:13:39.020
Then it uploads
that to GCS, using

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:42.680
the [? shaw ?] of the
commit as the slug for it.

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:44.660
And then it puts
a comment in that

00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:46.220
says, hey, go to this link.

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:48.410
And you'll have a staging
version of the site.

00:13:48.410 --> 00:13:49.160
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:51.493
MYLES BORINS: This just makes
it really easy for people.

00:13:51.493 --> 00:13:53.570
When they send PRs,
we can kick off a

00:13:53.570 --> 00:13:56.000
build, once we've reviewed
it and see that there's

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:57.260
nothing nefarious going on.

00:13:57.260 --> 00:13:58.780
[EVIL LAUGH TRACK]

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:00.740
And at that point,
we have a link

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:02.630
that anyone can
go to and look at.

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:05.060
So people can review
it, even on mobile.

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:07.220
You never have to really
have an environment set up

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:08.000
to review it.

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:08.740
JON FOUST: It's awesome.

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:09.680
MARK MANDEL: That's super nice.

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:12.410
Actually, it sounds like Cloud
Build and GitHub integration's

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:14.480
improved since I
last looked at it.

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:16.760
MYLES BORINS: Yeah-- and
it's been really fun digging

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:19.040
into this as well,
because there's

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:21.680
all sorts of
unexpected edge cases

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:22.940
that I've been encountering.

00:14:22.940 --> 00:14:25.500
And it's been really great
working with the Cloud Build

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:26.000
team.

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:27.410
They've been super responsive.

00:14:27.410 --> 00:14:29.150
And they're really
interested in making

00:14:29.150 --> 00:14:30.650
a best-in-class
integration here.

00:14:30.650 --> 00:14:31.810
So it's been really fun.

00:14:31.810 --> 00:14:31.880
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:33.440
And I know you do
a lot of stuff,

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:36.243
as well, the Node.js
Foundation side.

00:14:36.243 --> 00:14:38.660
There's been some interesting
foundational work that seems

00:14:38.660 --> 00:14:39.590
to have been going on recently.

00:14:39.590 --> 00:14:40.700
You want to tell us about
what's happening there?

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:41.660
MYLES BORINS: I like
how you called it.

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:42.410
It's foundational.

00:14:42.410 --> 00:14:43.370
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:46.760
So the Node.js Foundation
is a non-profit.

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:48.140
It's a 501(c)(6).

00:14:48.140 --> 00:14:51.800
And it has this
mission of sponsoring--

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:55.700
overseeing the Node.js project
and the work towards it.

00:14:55.700 --> 00:14:58.430
There's another foundation
in the ecosystem focused

00:14:58.430 --> 00:15:00.470
on JavaScript, called the JSF.

00:15:00.470 --> 00:15:01.820
It's the JS Foundation.

00:15:01.820 --> 00:15:05.360
And they are actually both
under the Linux Foundation,

00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:07.820
as where they were brought up.

00:15:07.820 --> 00:15:11.090
And for a number of different
reasons, including-- hey,

00:15:11.090 --> 00:15:14.240
there's two things, which one
do I support financially?--

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:17.900
we found that it was in the best
interest of all of the projects

00:15:17.900 --> 00:15:20.760
to look into merging
the two foundations.

00:15:20.760 --> 00:15:24.260
So last October, we
announced at Node Interactive

00:15:24.260 --> 00:15:26.680
that we had an intent
to merge the Node.js

00:15:26.680 --> 00:15:30.420
Foundation and the JSF into
one umbrella organization.

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:32.870
Some of the biggest differences
for the Node.js Foundation

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:36.560
itself was that there are
more than one projects in it.

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:39.200
Actually, Express and libuv are
under the Node.js Foundation

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:40.080
as well.

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:42.622
It was mostly built up to
support a single project.

00:15:42.622 --> 00:15:45.080
And the top-level governance
of that project, the Technical

00:15:45.080 --> 00:15:46.940
Steering Committee,
was chartered directly

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:50.720
from the board to oversee
everything in the open source

00:15:50.720 --> 00:15:51.460
side of things.

00:15:51.460 --> 00:15:53.160
There's a strong separation.

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:55.850
And I think we dug into
this in our last podcast.

00:15:55.850 --> 00:15:57.650
But there a really
strong separation

00:15:57.650 --> 00:16:01.160
between the technical governance
and community governance

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:02.560
and the financial governance.

00:16:02.560 --> 00:16:04.490
So the board oversees finances.

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:07.020
They oversee certain
degrees of marketing.

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:10.075
But the board is unable to tell
Node Foundation-- like, hey,

00:16:10.075 --> 00:16:11.450
put this person
on your committee

00:16:11.450 --> 00:16:13.370
or, hey, land this PR.

00:16:13.370 --> 00:16:16.070
We have a community governance
process that's set up.

00:16:16.070 --> 00:16:17.360
We're openly governed.

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:18.720
And that's how things run.

00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:20.840
And so one of the interesting
challenges here is--

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:23.747
the JSF has, I think,
15 or 20 projects.

00:16:23.747 --> 00:16:24.830
I should know that number.

00:16:24.830 --> 00:16:25.850
I don't know it off hand.

00:16:25.850 --> 00:16:26.600
MARK MANDEL: Give or take--

00:16:26.600 --> 00:16:27.260
MYLES BORINS:
Yeah-- give or take.

00:16:27.260 --> 00:16:27.990
MARK MANDEL: 10%.

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:29.360
MYLES BORINS: But in
the merger, we now

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:30.943
are going to have a
structure where we

00:16:30.943 --> 00:16:32.840
need to support many projects.

00:16:32.840 --> 00:16:36.200
And so since October,
we have been holding

00:16:36.200 --> 00:16:38.090
weekly open public meetings.

00:16:38.090 --> 00:16:39.933
We've been broadcasting
them on YouTube.

00:16:39.933 --> 00:16:40.850
And they're all saved.

00:16:40.850 --> 00:16:42.595
And there's meeting
notes for all of them

00:16:42.595 --> 00:16:43.720
that are on a GitHub repo--

00:16:43.720 --> 00:16:46.990
GitHub.com/nodejs/bootstrap.

00:16:46.990 --> 00:16:50.122
And this bootstrap committee has
been designing the governance

00:16:50.122 --> 00:16:51.080
for the new foundation.

00:16:51.080 --> 00:16:53.540
It started with a couple of
weeks of actually designing

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:55.070
the governance
for the committee,

00:16:55.070 --> 00:16:58.450
so we could design the
governance for the foundation.

00:16:58.450 --> 00:17:02.060
And once we reached consensus
on a process, it actually--

00:17:02.060 --> 00:17:04.550
in a really amazing way--
unlocked this thing we had

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:08.750
never really considered before,
where the foundation itself--

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:11.750
we never really had a
process for how collaborators

00:17:11.750 --> 00:17:15.079
could come up with new
processes or new committees

00:17:15.079 --> 00:17:16.940
or new initiatives
that they wanted

00:17:16.940 --> 00:17:18.560
to run within the foundation.

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:21.030
So the first thing we did was
actually design this process.

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:22.030
It's a proposal process.

00:17:22.030 --> 00:17:25.430
It's based on how TC39 designs
the JavaScript language.

00:17:25.430 --> 00:17:26.660
But there's three phases--

00:17:26.660 --> 00:17:28.620
technically four, if
you consider stage 0.

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:30.578
But it's like, hey, here's
an idea of something

00:17:30.578 --> 00:17:31.910
we want to do-- is stage 0.

00:17:31.910 --> 00:17:35.985
Stage 1 is, hey, here's a rough
shape of how we want to do it.

00:17:35.985 --> 00:17:37.610
To get to stage 2,
you need to actually

00:17:37.610 --> 00:17:40.460
have all the documentation
put together and agreed

00:17:40.460 --> 00:17:42.300
upon by the community
that, hey, this is

00:17:42.300 --> 00:17:43.550
exactly how we want to run it.

00:17:43.550 --> 00:17:45.500
And at stage 2, it
goes to the board

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:48.440
or to whatever committee
will oversee it to approve

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:49.740
that, hey, we agree with this.

00:17:49.740 --> 00:17:52.340
And then it moves to stage 3,
which means it's initiated.

00:17:52.340 --> 00:17:54.500
So moving forward,
the Foundation

00:17:54.500 --> 00:17:57.050
will actually have a whole
thing in place for people who

00:17:57.050 --> 00:17:58.340
want to do new things with it.

00:17:58.340 --> 00:18:00.840
The Foundation is being designed
with a top-level committee,

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:02.360
known as the
Cross-Project Council.

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:05.240
There'll be representatives
from various different projects

00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:07.567
within the Foundation
itself on that committee.

00:18:07.567 --> 00:18:09.650
And that committee will
be chartered by the board,

00:18:09.650 --> 00:18:12.050
to oversee chartering
other projects,

00:18:12.050 --> 00:18:14.798
managing budget, code
of conduct escalations.

00:18:14.798 --> 00:18:17.090
We're actually looking at
having a foundation-wide code

00:18:17.090 --> 00:18:17.750
of conduct.

00:18:17.750 --> 00:18:19.940
And it's going to be
interesting finding the balance

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:22.070
because, in the same
way that we kept

00:18:22.070 --> 00:18:23.810
a clear separation
between the board

00:18:23.810 --> 00:18:26.030
and the technical project
and the Node Foundation,

00:18:26.030 --> 00:18:28.265
we want to still maintain
a pretty clear separation

00:18:28.265 --> 00:18:29.640
between the
Cross-Project Council

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:31.020
and the technical projects.

00:18:31.020 --> 00:18:35.220
We don't want the foundation
itself dictating too directly

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:36.727
how projects should be run.

00:18:36.727 --> 00:18:38.310
But with that being
said, we're pretty

00:18:38.310 --> 00:18:40.800
opinionated about what it
means to be openly governed

00:18:40.800 --> 00:18:43.750
and what makes healthy,
active projects.

00:18:43.750 --> 00:18:46.000
So there are things
that will be necessary.

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:47.792
But we're trying to
find the balance there.

00:18:47.792 --> 00:18:49.750
So like, you will have
to have a license.

00:18:49.750 --> 00:18:51.667
It may have to be within
a subset of licenses.

00:18:51.667 --> 00:18:53.125
But we're not going
to be like, you

00:18:53.125 --> 00:18:54.430
must have the Apache license.

00:18:54.430 --> 00:18:55.847
But it will be
like, hey, you must

00:18:55.847 --> 00:18:59.130
have Apache or MIT or a GPL that
doesn't start with the letter

00:18:59.130 --> 00:19:01.510
A or some combination of that.

00:19:01.510 --> 00:19:03.840
We're also going to have
a strict code of conduct

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:06.480
that all of the
projects must adopt.

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:08.610
But then that introduces
interesting problems.

00:19:08.610 --> 00:19:10.710
Like, if a project only
has 10 or 15 people,

00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:14.562
how do we actually help
them maintain and enforce

00:19:14.562 --> 00:19:16.020
that code of conduct,
if they don't

00:19:16.020 --> 00:19:17.563
have the resources to do so?

00:19:17.563 --> 00:19:19.980
So we're looking up spinning
up foundation-wide committees

00:19:19.980 --> 00:19:20.877
to help with that.

00:19:20.877 --> 00:19:22.710
And there'll be a number
of different places

00:19:22.710 --> 00:19:25.170
where projects can come
in different progression.

00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:27.068
So for example,
right now, when we

00:19:27.068 --> 00:19:28.860
were doing the merger
of the libuv project,

00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:31.470
then that's the underlying
abstraction of system calls

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:32.760
that's used in node.

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:34.680
That project is
healthy and active--

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:37.560
but don't have any interest
in working with higher level

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:40.740
governance or getting any sort
of marketing support or larger

00:19:40.740 --> 00:19:41.565
financial support.

00:19:41.565 --> 00:19:43.440
They just want to keep
the lights on and have

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:44.810
infrastructure support.

00:19:44.810 --> 00:19:47.310
So we have a way that they can
come into the new foundation.

00:19:47.310 --> 00:19:48.870
There's no expectation
of them having

00:19:48.870 --> 00:19:50.430
to grow to do anything more.

00:19:50.430 --> 00:19:53.483
It's a vendor neutral place
for them to keep their IP.

00:19:53.483 --> 00:19:54.900
It's somewhere for
them to receive

00:19:54.900 --> 00:19:56.910
the small amount of
resources that they need

00:19:56.910 --> 00:19:58.620
and to just do their thing.

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:01.230
And they'll still be
empowered to come--

00:20:01.230 --> 00:20:03.940
like, any member of any
project in the Foundation

00:20:03.940 --> 00:20:06.120
will be able to
attend CPC meetings

00:20:06.120 --> 00:20:08.940
and be able to participate
in consensus at the CPC.

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:11.730
But there will be a smaller
subset of voting members

00:20:11.730 --> 00:20:13.830
that represent the impact
level projects, which

00:20:13.830 --> 00:20:15.307
is, like, our top level.

00:20:15.307 --> 00:20:16.890
I mentioned all these
different things

00:20:16.890 --> 00:20:18.265
you need to be in
the Foundation.

00:20:18.265 --> 00:20:21.390
There'll be even more guidelines
for projects that we're--

00:20:21.390 --> 00:20:24.090
impact level that will have more
support and from the Foundation

00:20:24.090 --> 00:20:24.720
itself.

00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:26.238
I think I spiraled out there.

00:20:26.238 --> 00:20:27.582
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:29.380 --> 00:20:30.750
There's lots of stuff.

00:20:30.750 --> 00:20:34.170
So I guess, in short, check
out that bootstrap repo.

00:20:34.170 --> 00:20:36.180
All of the governance
has been designed

00:20:36.180 --> 00:20:38.850
using that proposal process
that I was talking about.

00:20:38.850 --> 00:20:41.400
All the proposals are
documented in there.

00:20:41.400 --> 00:20:42.390
And you can read them.

00:20:42.390 --> 00:20:44.670
You can see the history of
how we got where we are.

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:47.010
And if you are a member
of any of the projects

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:48.720
that are involved
in the Foundation,

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:52.570
you're encouraged to open a PR
and start engaging and changing

00:20:52.570 --> 00:20:53.070
it.

00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:55.600
Show up to any of the
meetings and participate.

00:20:55.600 --> 00:20:57.840
We're trying to minimize
the amount of hierarchy,

00:20:57.840 --> 00:20:59.760
but create enough
structure to empower people

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:01.587
to enact the change
they want to see.

00:21:01.587 --> 00:21:03.420
JON FOUST: We've actually
shared a cab ride.

00:21:03.420 --> 00:21:06.220
And I remember you giving me
the less long-winded version

00:21:06.220 --> 00:21:06.720
of this.

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:08.845
And I can tell that you're
pretty excited about it.

00:21:08.845 --> 00:21:10.192
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:10.690 --> 00:21:13.190
MYLES BORINS: And this version
is still pretty abridged too.

00:21:13.190 --> 00:21:15.107
We could probably do,
like, a two-hour podcast

00:21:15.107 --> 00:21:16.380
of me talking just about this.

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:17.010
MARK MANDEL: Oh, my god.

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:18.968
JON FOUST: Definitely--
but Myles, I'm curious.

00:21:18.968 --> 00:21:21.090
What does this actually
mean for developers?

00:21:21.090 --> 00:21:23.928
MYLES BORINS: Foundations can
often be like a release team.

00:21:23.928 --> 00:21:25.470
And it's one of
those things that you

00:21:25.470 --> 00:21:26.940
don't notice until you need it.

00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:29.220
And generally, when it's
in a place that really

00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:31.830
is like, oh, my god, I'm so
glad I have this in place,

00:21:31.830 --> 00:21:34.800
it's usually too late
if you didn't have it.

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:36.240
A good thing for
developers would

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:38.160
be-- like, any of the
projects that are inside

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:42.000
of this foundation, they should
know that resources, oversight,

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:44.580
and initiatives are in place
to help support that project

00:21:44.580 --> 00:21:46.800
and make sure that
project is healthy.

00:21:46.800 --> 00:21:50.130
Arguably, if any of the
projects within the Foundation

00:21:50.130 --> 00:21:52.230
are not doing well,
that reflects poorly

00:21:52.230 --> 00:21:55.090
on the Foundation and the
Cross-Project Council itself.

00:21:55.090 --> 00:21:58.290
So I think that there is a
certain amount of comfort

00:21:58.290 --> 00:22:00.720
that people could have in
knowing that the projects are

00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:02.310
going to be maintained.

00:22:02.310 --> 00:22:04.080
The other side,
too, is, if you're

00:22:04.080 --> 00:22:05.790
a developer working
for a company

00:22:05.790 --> 00:22:09.000
and that company, maybe, has
stringent open-source policies

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:11.400
or, alternatively, if you
want to be collaborating

00:22:11.400 --> 00:22:14.910
on something, in general,
having the IP of an open source

00:22:14.910 --> 00:22:19.560
project in a neutral body
that's a nonprofit significantly

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:22.290
helps organizations collaborate
together on these things.

00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:27.210
So Node.js being inside of
this nonprofit in this vendor

00:22:27.210 --> 00:22:29.130
neutral place to
store everything--

00:22:29.130 --> 00:22:32.280
that allows companies like
Google and Microsoft and IBM

00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:35.730
and Intel all to come
together and collaborate.

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:38.280
And I know at least
from personal experience

00:22:38.280 --> 00:22:40.800
that some of the
organizations that I mentioned

00:22:40.800 --> 00:22:43.380
would not put an entire
headcount or multiple

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:46.470
headcount onto a project if
it was owned by a competitor.

00:22:46.470 --> 00:22:48.670
That's just not smart business.

00:22:48.670 --> 00:22:51.490
So I think, also,
as a developer,

00:22:51.490 --> 00:22:53.430
it helps you to maybe
contribute back,

00:22:53.430 --> 00:22:55.710
depending on the company
that you work for

00:22:55.710 --> 00:22:56.550
and their policies.

00:22:56.550 --> 00:22:58.000
JON FOUST: Nice-- awesome.

00:22:58.000 --> 00:22:59.250
MARK MANDEL: Nice, nice, nice.

00:22:59.250 --> 00:23:00.780
What news have we
got about Node.js

00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:01.920
on Google Cloud Platform?

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:04.080
Have we had any recent
updates or cool things

00:23:04.080 --> 00:23:06.400
that have come out that you
want to talk about as well?

00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:07.440
MYLES BORINS: Yeah-- absolutely.

00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:09.065
There's a couple
different places where

00:23:09.065 --> 00:23:10.488
you can run Node in our cloud.

00:23:10.488 --> 00:23:12.030
MARK MANDEL: Yeah--
maybe give people

00:23:12.030 --> 00:23:13.860
a reminder of where you
can run Node in our cloud.

00:23:13.860 --> 00:23:15.443
That's probably a
good starting point.

00:23:15.443 --> 00:23:17.610
MYLES BORINS: One of
the obvious ones--

00:23:17.610 --> 00:23:20.910
like, the old-school way of
running code in Google Cloud--

00:23:20.910 --> 00:23:22.260
is App Engine.

00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:25.710
And so we've got both App Engine
Flex and App Engine Standard

00:23:25.710 --> 00:23:27.330
Support for Node.js.

00:23:27.330 --> 00:23:30.000
We currently support
the Node.js 8 runtime

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:31.360
in general availability.

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:33.640
And then the 10 runtime is beta.

00:23:33.640 --> 00:23:35.170
But I do believe
that we're going

00:23:35.170 --> 00:23:38.950
to be moving 10 to general
availability in short order.

00:23:38.950 --> 00:23:40.910
I don't have an
explicit timeline on it.

00:23:40.910 --> 00:23:42.733
But sooner rather
than later might

00:23:42.733 --> 00:23:43.900
be a nice way of putting it.

00:23:43.900 --> 00:23:44.900
MARK MANDEL: That works.

00:23:44.900 --> 00:23:47.320
MYLES BORINS: For Cloud
Functions, we support Node.js.

00:23:47.320 --> 00:23:50.110
We support Node.js
in version 6 and 8.

00:23:50.110 --> 00:23:52.480
Both of those are in
general availability.

00:23:52.480 --> 00:23:54.340
And in a similar
short order, we're

00:23:54.340 --> 00:23:58.420
looking at bringing out the
Node.js 10 runtime in beta

00:23:58.420 --> 00:24:00.250
on Cloud Functions.

00:24:00.250 --> 00:24:02.310
We also have this thing
that's being worked on.

00:24:02.310 --> 00:24:04.060
It's kind of secret,
but not super secret.

00:24:04.060 --> 00:24:06.100
Because I know I can
tweak the sign-up link.

00:24:06.100 --> 00:24:07.475
But we have this
thing that we've

00:24:07.475 --> 00:24:09.630
been working on called
serverless containers.

00:24:09.630 --> 00:24:12.730
And that's totally a place
that you can run Node as well.

00:24:12.730 --> 00:24:14.320
Arguably, you can
run anything that's

00:24:14.320 --> 00:24:17.030
in a Docker container that
responds to something on port

00:24:17.030 --> 00:24:17.530
80.

00:24:17.530 --> 00:24:18.808
[LAUGH TRACK]

00:24:19.660 --> 00:24:21.280
But it is a cool
place to put Node.

00:24:21.280 --> 00:24:24.430
And one of the ways I like
to think about it too is--

00:24:24.430 --> 00:24:26.960
all of these are
abstractions on themselves.

00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:29.920
And I think something that
we're working towards and trying

00:24:29.920 --> 00:24:31.870
to get out in the
Node 10 runtime

00:24:31.870 --> 00:24:34.660
for Cloud Functions is
this idea that there really

00:24:34.660 --> 00:24:37.810
isn't that much difference
between a container, an app,

00:24:37.810 --> 00:24:38.590
and a function.

00:24:38.590 --> 00:24:42.220
And a function itself is
built with containers.

00:24:42.220 --> 00:24:45.140
And it just has an opinionated
way of running the code.

00:24:45.140 --> 00:24:46.360
It looks a lot like Express.

00:24:46.360 --> 00:24:48.790
But it's a little framework
that's built on top of Node

00:24:48.790 --> 00:24:50.780
to make it easier
to run your code.

00:24:50.780 --> 00:24:53.500
So if you're writing
code inside of a function

00:24:53.500 --> 00:24:56.890
and then you hit this boundary
of, like, hey, working

00:24:56.890 --> 00:24:59.170
within this one function
isn't really giving me

00:24:59.170 --> 00:25:02.050
all the flexibility and
expressiveness that I need,

00:25:02.050 --> 00:25:04.300
you can easily take that
and turn it into an app

00:25:04.300 --> 00:25:08.200
and then deploy it to App
Engine and see similar billing

00:25:08.200 --> 00:25:10.700
or similar functionality--

00:25:10.700 --> 00:25:12.708
also maybe better
cold start times,

00:25:12.708 --> 00:25:14.000
depending on what you're doing.

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:15.583
But if you hit the
edge of, like, hey,

00:25:15.583 --> 00:25:18.940
this is the edge of what I can
do with App Engine Standard--

00:25:18.940 --> 00:25:21.400
which is going to have faster
deploy times and it's more

00:25:21.400 --> 00:25:22.210
managed--

00:25:22.210 --> 00:25:24.640
you can move to Flex or
to serverless containers

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:26.640
and start using a
Docker container,

00:25:26.640 --> 00:25:29.202
if maybe there's a native
module that we don't support

00:25:29.202 --> 00:25:30.910
or there's something
that you need to do,

00:25:30.910 --> 00:25:32.710
like spinning up
a certain binary,

00:25:32.710 --> 00:25:34.750
or anything fancy
that goes beyond what

00:25:34.750 --> 00:25:36.310
we offer in our base image.

00:25:36.310 --> 00:25:37.810
One of the things
that I find really

00:25:37.810 --> 00:25:39.643
empowering and important
to think about is--

00:25:39.643 --> 00:25:42.440
there are containers all the
way down all of these things.

00:25:42.440 --> 00:25:44.830
And so it's just
like, how much sugar

00:25:44.830 --> 00:25:46.090
do you want in your cereal?

00:25:46.090 --> 00:25:48.348
And how much do you want
things to be on rails?

00:25:48.348 --> 00:25:48.890
I don't know.

00:25:48.890 --> 00:25:50.390
I can come up with
some more idioms.

00:25:50.390 --> 00:25:50.980
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:50.980 --> 00:25:52.040
JON FOUST: Sugar in
your cereal's awesome.

00:25:52.040 --> 00:25:53.710
MYLES BORINS: But really,
at the end of the day,

00:25:53.710 --> 00:25:55.180
I guess functions also
have the advantage

00:25:55.180 --> 00:25:56.330
of eventing right now.

00:25:56.330 --> 00:25:58.840
So you can take some event
and tie it to a function.

00:25:58.840 --> 00:26:00.518
So it's really easy
to have something

00:26:00.518 --> 00:26:02.560
that puts a whole bunch
of things in a task queue

00:26:02.560 --> 00:26:04.780
that triggers a Pub/Sub
and then triggers

00:26:04.780 --> 00:26:06.220
a function in the background.

00:26:06.220 --> 00:26:07.637
I like using these
sort of things,

00:26:07.637 --> 00:26:11.270
like emailers or for
transcoding and stuff like that.

00:26:11.270 --> 00:26:13.420
But overall, all of
these different things

00:26:13.420 --> 00:26:14.710
have their uses.

00:26:14.710 --> 00:26:17.410
And we can grow
with you, depending

00:26:17.410 --> 00:26:19.840
on what you're doing,
how you're doing it,

00:26:19.840 --> 00:26:21.098
and what you need to do.

00:26:21.098 --> 00:26:22.140
MARK MANDEL: Yeah-- sure.

00:26:22.140 --> 00:26:26.260
So Myles, what are the places
can we actually run JavaScript?

00:26:26.260 --> 00:26:29.020
MYLES BORINS: So there are
a handful of other places

00:26:29.020 --> 00:26:31.600
you can run JavaScript in
Google Cloud that's really fun.

00:26:31.600 --> 00:26:33.340
And maybe you wouldn't
think about it.

00:26:33.340 --> 00:26:37.480
You can run JavaScript inside
of any G Suite products

00:26:37.480 --> 00:26:38.470
with Apps Script.

00:26:38.470 --> 00:26:40.300
Apps Script is JavaScript.

00:26:40.300 --> 00:26:42.940
And there's a new beta that
you can use to try it out

00:26:42.940 --> 00:26:44.350
with the new V8 engine.

00:26:44.350 --> 00:26:47.400
So if you've ever
written functions to--

00:26:47.400 --> 00:26:49.270
like, edit your spreadsheets--

00:26:49.270 --> 00:26:50.950
I'm actually using it right now.

00:26:50.950 --> 00:26:53.080
We're managing a guest
list for a large event

00:26:53.080 --> 00:26:54.200
that we're planning.

00:26:54.200 --> 00:26:56.410
And there were some
queries that we

00:26:56.410 --> 00:26:58.180
want to do that I
couldn't figure out how

00:26:58.180 --> 00:27:00.910
to do that inside of Sheets.

00:27:00.910 --> 00:27:02.920
So I actually just
wrote a little script.

00:27:02.920 --> 00:27:05.320
And it did it in
a really nice way.

00:27:05.320 --> 00:27:08.540
You can also write user-defined
functions inside a BigQuery.

00:27:08.540 --> 00:27:10.540
So you can write JavaScript
instead of BigQuery,

00:27:10.540 --> 00:27:14.380
in case your JavaScript
skills are a little bit better

00:27:14.380 --> 00:27:17.570
than your SQL skills.

00:27:17.570 --> 00:27:19.900
Another one that you can do
there that I find super fun

00:27:19.900 --> 00:27:22.600
is-- like, if you're doing
anything with parsing JSON,

00:27:22.600 --> 00:27:25.570
it's way easier to parse and
manipulate JSON with JavaScript

00:27:25.570 --> 00:27:26.920
than it is with SQL.

00:27:26.920 --> 00:27:30.222
So you can write little
query functions to do that.

00:27:30.222 --> 00:27:31.930
MARK MANDEL: I don't
know Node very well.

00:27:31.930 --> 00:27:33.970
And we're talking about
different Node versions.

00:27:33.970 --> 00:27:36.700
Like you said, Apps Script
is moving to the V8 runtime.

00:27:36.700 --> 00:27:41.230
And on App Engine, it was
moving from, like, 8 to 10

00:27:41.230 --> 00:27:41.890
or something.

00:27:41.890 --> 00:27:45.760
Why would I want to change
the different versions of Node

00:27:45.760 --> 00:27:46.600
that I'd be using?

00:27:46.600 --> 00:27:48.310
Why do I care as a developer?

00:27:48.310 --> 00:27:49.280
MYLES BORINS: One
thing I could advise

00:27:49.280 --> 00:27:50.560
to people who are
listening, if they

00:27:50.560 --> 00:27:51.970
want to learn more about this--

00:27:51.970 --> 00:27:55.390
there's a talk that I
gave called "The Hilarious

00:27:55.390 --> 00:27:57.520
Misadventures of
Being a Platform

00:27:57.520 --> 00:28:00.190
Downstream from your
Language" that digs

00:28:00.190 --> 00:28:01.480
into this a little bit.

00:28:01.480 --> 00:28:05.710
But the shorter version is,
Node conflates the concept

00:28:05.710 --> 00:28:07.930
of an API and a runtime.

00:28:07.930 --> 00:28:09.820
And it embeds
something called V8.

00:28:09.820 --> 00:28:12.603
So V8 is the JavaScript engine
from the Chrome browser.

00:28:12.603 --> 00:28:14.020
It's part of the
Chromium Project.

00:28:14.020 --> 00:28:15.690
It's its own open
source project.

00:28:15.690 --> 00:28:18.860
And V8 gets embedded in a whole
bunch of different places.

00:28:18.860 --> 00:28:21.460
So when we're talking
about Apps Script,

00:28:21.460 --> 00:28:23.570
the new version is based on V8.

00:28:23.570 --> 00:28:27.130
When we're talking about those
user-defined function stuff,

00:28:27.130 --> 00:28:29.060
V8 is what's executing
the JavaScript.

00:28:29.060 --> 00:28:32.110
So V8 is a JavaScript
virtual machine.

00:28:32.110 --> 00:28:33.860
And it gets embedded
in different places--

00:28:33.860 --> 00:28:36.110
Chromium being one of them
and Node being one of them.

00:28:36.110 --> 00:28:36.680
We embed V8.

00:28:36.680 --> 00:28:38.570
When we execute your
JavaScript code,

00:28:38.570 --> 00:28:40.610
we're handing it off to that VM.

00:28:40.610 --> 00:28:43.310
And it's executing it
and doing stuff with it.

00:28:43.310 --> 00:28:45.680
But with Node, it
executes your code.

00:28:45.680 --> 00:28:48.680
But it's not just executing
arbitrary JavaScript.

00:28:48.680 --> 00:28:52.940
It's executing it with
very specific semantics.

00:28:52.940 --> 00:28:56.150
Like, when you use Require,
for example, that is a Nodism.

00:28:56.150 --> 00:28:59.120
That's an API that we
created for module loading.

00:28:59.120 --> 00:29:00.980
And when you use
something like FS,

00:29:00.980 --> 00:29:04.035
that's not this standardized
API for File System work

00:29:04.035 --> 00:29:04.910
that runs everywhere.

00:29:04.910 --> 00:29:07.430
It is a Node-specific API.

00:29:07.430 --> 00:29:09.350
So Node itself is
that combination

00:29:09.350 --> 00:29:11.813
of a runtime and an API surface.

00:29:11.813 --> 00:29:13.730
And when we talk about
the different versions,

00:29:13.730 --> 00:29:17.570
like Node 8 versus Node 10, one
of the biggest and most obvious

00:29:17.570 --> 00:29:20.607
differences is that 10 has a
newer version of the 8 in it.

00:29:20.607 --> 00:29:21.440
MARK MANDEL: Gotcha.

00:29:21.440 --> 00:29:23.107
MYLES BORINS: Because
it came out later.

00:29:23.107 --> 00:29:26.030
And us updating V8 is a
[INAUDIBLE] major change

00:29:26.030 --> 00:29:27.930
for all sorts of
different reasons.

00:29:27.930 --> 00:29:29.900
I gave another talk
called "Node.js Versions--

00:29:29.900 --> 00:29:30.960
How do they Work?--

00:29:30.960 --> 00:29:32.543
which digs into that.

00:29:32.543 --> 00:29:34.460
But essentially, it's a
breaking change for us

00:29:34.460 --> 00:29:36.770
to upgrade V8 inside of Node.

00:29:36.770 --> 00:29:39.350
So when you're on an older
version of Node, like Node 8,

00:29:39.350 --> 00:29:42.480
you're using an older version
of the virtual machine.

00:29:42.480 --> 00:29:45.170
So it may not have all the
same language features.

00:29:45.170 --> 00:29:48.350
I think one of the ones
that's in 10 that isn't in 8

00:29:48.350 --> 00:29:52.250
is, like, array.includes, maybe.

00:29:52.250 --> 00:29:53.343
There's a couple of them.

00:29:53.343 --> 00:29:55.010
The JavaScript language
is standardized.

00:29:55.010 --> 00:29:57.297
And there's new features
added on a yearly basis.

00:29:57.297 --> 00:29:59.630
So a new version of Node is
likely to have new features.

00:29:59.630 --> 00:30:02.840
Like, Node 8, for example,
introduced async await,

00:30:02.840 --> 00:30:05.382
which is a new way of handling
asynchronicity, which I can

00:30:05.382 --> 00:30:06.590
babble about for a long time.

00:30:06.590 --> 00:30:07.130
MARK MANDEL: Ha!

00:30:07.130 --> 00:30:08.755
MYLES BORINS: But
Node 10, for example,

00:30:08.755 --> 00:30:11.900
comes with a whole bunch of
optimizations to async await.

00:30:11.900 --> 00:30:14.210
So the same async await
code will run, like,

00:30:14.210 --> 00:30:17.120
20% faster in Node 10 than
in Node 8, because when

00:30:17.120 --> 00:30:20.180
it came out in Node 8, it was
still a relatively new feature.

00:30:20.180 --> 00:30:21.763
That's the different
versions of Node.

00:30:21.763 --> 00:30:23.847
But then there's also all
those different versions

00:30:23.847 --> 00:30:25.640
of V8, which introduce
the new features.

00:30:25.640 --> 00:30:27.290
And when we upgrade
V8 in Node, we

00:30:27.290 --> 00:30:29.450
get those new features in
Node, which then in turn

00:30:29.450 --> 00:30:33.840
requires us to update some
of our APIs to reflect that.

00:30:33.840 --> 00:30:35.510
So we're in the
process, for example,

00:30:35.510 --> 00:30:38.120
of introducing a whole bunch
of new promise-based APIs.

00:30:38.120 --> 00:30:41.120
Because most of our APIs are
traditionally callback-based

00:30:41.120 --> 00:30:42.200
and-- more specifically--

00:30:42.200 --> 00:30:45.050
Node callback based, which
is a very specific way

00:30:45.050 --> 00:30:46.650
of writing callback code.

00:30:46.650 --> 00:30:49.040
But if you want to use async
await, you want promises.

00:30:49.040 --> 00:30:51.530
So like, for example,
in Node 8, we

00:30:51.530 --> 00:30:54.110
have this new API
called util.promisify.

00:30:54.110 --> 00:30:55.880
So you could take any
API that was written

00:30:55.880 --> 00:30:58.100
using a callback-based API.

00:30:58.100 --> 00:31:00.338
We turn it into a
promise-based API for you.

00:31:00.338 --> 00:31:01.880
But one of the things
that we can do,

00:31:01.880 --> 00:31:04.880
as a runtime, because we're
directly interfacing with V8,

00:31:04.880 --> 00:31:09.560
is we can call the underlying
mechanisms of creating promises

00:31:09.560 --> 00:31:10.730
in V8 itself.

00:31:10.730 --> 00:31:14.570
So we can actually remove,
like, one promise and a couple

00:31:14.570 --> 00:31:17.540
stack frames by
doing it this way.

00:31:17.540 --> 00:31:19.850
So if you use our
util.promisify function

00:31:19.850 --> 00:31:22.773
to turn your callback APIs
into promise-based APIs,

00:31:22.773 --> 00:31:24.440
it'll be more efficient
than if you just

00:31:24.440 --> 00:31:26.060
wrapped it in a promise.

00:31:26.060 --> 00:31:28.293
But people can also just
write promise-based APIs.

00:31:28.293 --> 00:31:29.960
There's lots of
different ways to do it.

00:31:29.960 --> 00:31:32.727
To your original question
of why that matters--

00:31:32.727 --> 00:31:35.060
really, it comes down to what
API surface you want to do

00:31:35.060 --> 00:31:36.977
and what language features
you want available.

