Visual Studio as your editor

00:33:57.190 --> 00:33:59.950
for Unity, what you do is you
download the NuGet package.

00:33:59.950 --> 00:34:03.010
And it will download a series
of libraries on your computer.

00:34:03.010 --> 00:34:05.260
And you would actually
have to locate those files

00:34:05.260 --> 00:34:08.050
and move them over to a plugin
folder in your Unity project's

00:34:08.050 --> 00:34:08.780
root folder.

00:34:08.780 --> 00:34:10.780
Once you do that, you
have to create this link--

00:34:10.780 --> 00:34:12.433
that XML file-- that
actually loads all

00:34:12.433 --> 00:34:13.600
these references on startup.

00:34:13.600 --> 00:34:15.310
Because just download
the NuGet packages

00:34:15.310 --> 00:34:17.260
won't really help
you, because Unity

00:34:17.260 --> 00:34:19.900
looks for those dependencies
and those references and all

00:34:19.900 --> 00:34:22.395
those assemblies that you need
to load up every single time

00:34:22.395 --> 00:34:23.270
that you start Unity.

00:34:23.270 --> 00:34:26.380
And if your project cannot have
a file that points to those,

00:34:26.380 --> 00:34:27.520
then it won't start up.

00:34:27.520 --> 00:34:29.949
So that's a great option
to help you get started.

